<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mauricio Hochschild</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1881–1965</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mauricio Hochschild was a German-born mining entrepreneur who became one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>'s three '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> barons', alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-patino">Simón Patiño</a> and the Aramayo family. His company controlled a large share of Bolivian tin and other mineral exports until the nationalisation of the great mines in 1952. In the late 1930s he helped thousands of Jewish refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to settle in Bolivia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mauricio-hochschild.jpg" alt="Mauricio Hochschild" width="320" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Irene Bran · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1026470_F82658.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
